ted resources on mastering the three  essential things required to launch a  transportation Network finding Riders  finding drivers and building the app the  first one was easy Marcus had access to  his school email list so he sent out a  Google form survey and got over 600  people to respond most people agreed  that existing taxi services sucked and  joined the waitlist to be notified when  bolt launched signing up drivers was  harder every day after school Marcus  would go visit taxi ranks and try to  convince drivers to sign up getting them  to say yes was extremely difficult the  taxi drivers were resistant to any  technological change most of them didn't  even have a smartphone yet Marcus  probably holds the world record for the  number of taxis that he has been thrown  out of but even though Marcus heard  hundreds of no's he persisted finally  after months of speaking to almost every  taxi driver in Estonia Marcus succeeded  in signing up the first 50 drivers not  as much as he'd hoped for but enough to  gain initial traction the third piece of  the puzzle was technology Marcus  followed the Lean Startup Playbook he  signed up the Riders and drivers before  he actually committed to building the  app even though Marcus was more  technically skilled than 99 of his peers  he still didn't feel like he had the  engineering chops needed to build the  app himself so he borrowed 5000 year  heroes from his parents to hire a  freelance developer 5000 Euros might not  sound like a lot of money especially if  you're from Switzerland or San Francisco  but remember this was 2013 Estonia where  the average salary was only around 700  euros Marcus's parents had been saving  this money for years to help fund his  university studies so when I was trying  to convince my parents to give me the  first couple of thousand Euros to get  going it took me many many weeks to  actually convince them to do it even  though that small investment would be  worth millions in just a few years 