missions from drivers  while offering better prices to Riders  whenever the leading payer pushes  margins to be too high starts really  abusing their position with drivers it  actually creates an opening for other  players to come in major ride sharing  companies were charging 30 to 40 percent  commissions from drivers while bolt took  only 15 to 20 percent in some cases we  even take about half the level that our  competition does and why that's even  possible is because from from the start  we've been a very lean company after  building a strong foothold in Eastern  Europe both decided to expand into  another Market ignored by most  competitors Africa this was initially a  counter-intuitive decision we had zero  experience in Africa most of us said  they were never even been there but  Marcus is an extremely data driven  entrepreneur so he ignored conventional  wisdom and focused solely on The  Economic Opportunity and the numbers  didn't lie Africa's high unemployment  made it easy to sign up drivers while  poor public transport created strong  demand for ride sharing things that on  the surface looked like negatives  actually turned into positives for bolt  now one of the key reasons that both has  been successful is that Marcus is  extremely Frugal seriously he never  spends money on anything unless he is  certain that it creates more value for  the customer while American Tech  unicorns were chartering private jets  Marcus didn't want to waste money on an  800 plane ticket to South Africa to plan  his expansion so what did they do well  bolts started in South Africa with just  one local employee who they hired over  Skype from Estonia no one from the  headquarters actually traveled to the  country until almost a year later and  Bolt's first office had none of the  usual Tech startup office perks and when  bolt expanded to other African nations  including Nigeria Ghana Kenya Tanzania  Uganda and Tunisia they always local  lies the product rather than cars Bolt'