missions from drivers  while offering better prices to Riders  whenever the leading payer pushes  margins to be too high starts really  abusing their position with drivers it  actually creates an opening for other  players to come in major ride sharing  companies were charging 30 to 40 percent  commissions from drivers while bolt took  only 15 to 20 percent in some cases we  even take about half the level that our  competition does and why that's even  possible is because from from the start  we've been a very lean company after  building a strong foothold in Eastern  Europe both decided to expand into  another Market ignored by most  competitors Africa this was initially a  counter-intuitive decision we had zero  experience in Africa most of us said  they were never even been there but  Marcus is an extremely data driven  entrepreneur so he ignored conventional  wisdom and focused solely on The  Economic Opportunity and the numbers  didn't lie Africa's high unemployment  made it easy to sign up drivers while  poor public transport created strong  demand for ride sharing things that on  the surface looked like negatives  actually turned into positives for bolt  now one of the key reasons that both has  been successful is that Marcus is  extremely Frugal seriously he never  spends money on anything unless he is  certain that it creates more value for  the customer while American Tech  unicorns were chartering private jets  Marcus didn't want to waste money on an  800 plane ticket to South Africa to plan  his expansion so what did they do well  bolts started in South Africa with just  one local employee who they hired over  Skype from Estonia no one from the  headquarters actually traveled to the  country until almost a year later and  Bolt's first office had none of the  usual Tech startup office perks and when  bolt expanded to other African nations  including Nigeria Ghana Kenya Tanzania  Uganda and Tunisia they always local  lies the product rather than cars Bolt's  most popular category in Africa is  motorcycles both arrived in Africa one  year after Uber so you'd think that  Marcus had no chance of going up against  a much better funded competitor but as  Marcus researched Uber service he  realized the San francisco-based  ride-sharing Behemoth was making  mistakes which bolt was quick to  capitalize on Uber initially only  accepted credit cards even though only a  few percent of Africans actually had  credit cards at the time so bolt offered  locally popular payment methods like  cash payments and mobile carrier billing  and safety is a Hot Topic in Africa so  bolt added an SOS button to its app and  when drivers struggled to get car loans  from Banks it partnered with local  financing companies to help up to 200  000 new drivers get cars and start  driving for bolt today one third of both  businesses in Africa Marcus expanded to  Africa and was able to beat Uber even  though Uber was more than 10 times the  size of Bolt the lesson here is that you  should never be afraid to go up against  bigger companies especially if your name  is Marcus vilig in 2010 Daimler the  parent company of Mercedes-Benz made  what could have been the investment of  the century they paid 50 million dollars  for a 10 stake of an electric vehicle  startup named Tesla Motors today those  shares would be worth 77 billion dollars  unfortunately Daimler decided to sell  their Tesla stake just a few years later  so they missed out on those incredible  returns but the German automotive giant  seems to have learned from its mistakes  with Tesla in 2018 Daimler invested 150  million dollars in Bolt and remains one  of the company's largest shareholders to  this day this German automotive giant  gave up on one of the greatest  entrepreneurs of Our Generation so why  is it betting on a millennial University  Dropout from Estonia who still walks  three miles to work every morning well  Daimler might be the largest  manufacturer of luxury cars in the world  today but looking to the Future it knows  it has a problem today there are about  250 million private cars in Europe and  it's clear that 10 years from now now we  are going to have a lot fewer private  cars than we do today cities are  suffering from traffic congestion and  pollution no politician today is  thinking about how to make car ownership  and driving easier but a lot of  politicians across Europe are working to  make it more difficult they're  increasing fuel taxes introducing low  emission zones and City centers and  promoting public transport Daimler sees  this as a massive risk to their business  so they invested in both as a hedge now  a lot of people think that self-driving  cars are the solution to congestion and  pollution you might remember that Uber  invested billions of dollars into  developing autonomous vehicles a few  years ago but Marcus actually doesn't  believe that the technology will be  ready anytime soon it's not that Marcus  is burying his head in the sand Bolt's  Chief product officer has a PHD in  computer science but for now they are  focusing on the things that work today  not 10 years from now Marcus mentioned  three important things there public  transportation micro Mobility which is  simply an umbrella term for light  Vehicles like bicycles and electric  scooters and shared car  that's why in 2018 bolt entered the  micro Mobility space with the launch of  scooter sharing again it's a bit of an  unexpected move but bolt is already the  largest Micro Mobility company in Europe  it has 130 000 scooters today and is  adding 230 000 more this year one in  four Urban trips is less than two miles  scooters and bicycles can replace a lot  of these short car Journeys I mean even  the president of Estonia swapped out his  motorcade for a bolt scooter but there  are still over 250 million passenger  cars in Europe because of edge cases  like transporting furniture or traveling  to the countryside prevent many people  from giving up their personal cars  Marcus wants to help people give up  their cars entirely so in 2021 bolt  launched bolt drive a short-term car  rental service which allows you to rent  a car with fuel insurance and parking  included for as low as just 12 cents per  minute car sharing is Bolt's most  Capital intensive business which means  bolt can't scale this as fast as its  scooter Fleet for car sharing to become  as convenient as personal cars you need  to have a high density of Vehicles  available at basically every street  corner so every city requires massive  investment but Marcus clearly believes  that despite the high Capital  requirements the business model has  Merit this year bolt plans to expand  bolt drive into nine cities across  Europe one thing is for certain Marcus  isn't slowing down anytime soon remember  transportation is a massive industry  globally so bolt won't run out of growth  opportunities anytime soon Marcus wants  to build bolt into one of the largest  technology companies in Europe this  might sound audacious but Marcus  recently said that he increasingly  believes that the only company that  operates with the same strategy as Bolt  is Amazon both companies enter new  Industries and find ways to make them  significantly more efficient with  technology a lot of companies are good  at technology or operations but bold and  Amazon are good at both I'll leave you  with this in 1998 Amazon was a 600  million dollar Revenue company and its  billionaire founder drove a Honda Civic  to work in 2021 bolt was a 500 million  euro Revenue company and its billionaire  founder walked to work
 

